schema: spec-driven

# android-forge project rules for OpenSpec (rendered by /android-forge-init / /android-forge-adopt).
# `context` and `rules` steer every /opsx:propose; `operations` steer /opsx:apply and archive.
# Edit freely — this file is project policy. Conventions: android-forge docs/product-workflow.md.

context: |
  ${APP_NAME} is an Android app (Jetpack Compose, adaptive phone/tablet/foldable plus the form factors
  listed in CLAUDE.md) on the android-forge locked stack (Hilt, Orbit MVI state-only, Navigation 3 with
  api/impl feature modules, kotlin-result). The project CLAUDE.md and the android-forge docs it
  links are the source of truth for module layering, the stack and testing: a change follows them
  and never re-decides them (changing them is an ADR in docs/DECISIONS/ first).
  Work arrives as GitHub issues. A change implements exactly one issue; an issue labeled `epic` is
  split into one issue per slice before anything is proposed. docs/MVP_SPEC.md is product scope:
  a change outside it needs the scope decision recorded there first.

rules:
  proposal:
    - "Name the issue it implements on its own line: `Issue: #<n>` (or `Issue: none` for ad-hoc work)."
    - Stay inside that issue's scope. Copy its "Out of scope" list into a Non-goals section and never widen it.
    - One coherent slice that fits one pull request. If it does not fit, say how to split the issue instead of proposing all of it.
  specs:
    - Turn each acceptance criterion of the issue into at least one scenario.
    - Every requirement has at least one WHEN/THEN scenario that checks observable behavior (what the user or the caller sees), never implementation details.
    - Give scenarios short, unique names; tests and QA journeys refer to them by name.
  design:
    - "Place every new type in its android-forge module: entities in :core:model; use cases and repository interfaces in :core:domain; repository implementations in :core:data (internal) over data sources in :core:datastore / :core:database / :core:network; Nav 3 keys in :feature:<name>:api; screens, ViewModels, state and the @IntoSet entry builder in :feature:<name>:impl (all internal). A feature impl never depends on :core:data."
    - "Name every form factor the change touches (:app phone/tablet/foldable, :wear, :tv, :automotive, Auto, XR, glasses, desktop) and how its UI adapts; layout changes in :app cover compact and expanded windows."
    - Record any new library, platform API or cross-cutting decision as an ADR under docs/DECISIONS/ rather than deciding it silently.
  tasks:
    - Order tasks dependencies-first (:core:model → :core:domain → :core:data and its sources → :feature:*:api → :feature:*:impl → :app) and name the module each task touches.
    - "Every scenario gets a test task, and that test carries a `// Scenario: <scenario name>` comment so coverage can be traced. Tests use fakes from :core:testing and Orbit's test harness, never mocks of interfaces we own."
    - "Every new or changed screen gets a screen test and phone + tablet Roborazzi goldens (recordRoborazziDebug)."
    - "End with the local gate: ./gradlew spotlessApply && ./gradlew spotlessCheck lint coverageVerify assembleDebug -Proborazzi.test.verify=true"

operations:
  apply:
    guidance:
      - Tick each task in tasks.md as it is completed; the PR carries the ticked tasks.md.
      - "The pull request body says `Fixes #<n>` for the change's issue, so merging closes it."
  archive:
    guidance:
      - Archive after the implementation is merged, in its own docs(openspec) commit or PR.
